WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.940
Picture a child's drawing, just a single green leaf sitting inside a glass jar on a table because today we are diving into the absolute wildest idea from this whole project.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:19.460
Right. I mean, last time we found a way to grow a redesigned plant in raw Martian dirt, but, you know, that really only works if it stays safely inside a dome.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.080
And a dome is, well, it is really only the size of the room it stands in, but a true settlement needs actual fields, right?

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:38.500
Exactly. So the stretch goal now is to grow that same redesigned plant entirely outside on the open surface, like out there with nothing built around it at all.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:46.480
So just to place exactly where we are for you listening, this is version five of five. It is the last one and definitely the furthest from being finished.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:56.580
Yeah, because going out on the open surface takes away basically everything you take for granted. It strips away the air pressure, the warmth, the water, and any shelter from the sun's harsh or light.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:06.020
And that loss of air pressure is where the physics gets honestly pretty crazy. I mean, without that pressure, a plant physically cannot hold onto its water. Right.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:13.820
The water inside a warm leaf would not just dry out slowly out there. Because the air is so incredibly thin, it would actually boil away.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:15.440
Wait, really?

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:26.160
It just boils? Oh, absolutely. And it would do that at about the temperature of a cool spring day. So basically, a plant out there cannot be warm and open at the same time.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:35.460
Okay, but this is where Mars actually starts to help, which is totally wild, because below freezing that thin air is unusually rich in the gas plants breathe.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:48.160
Exactly. So if the leaf is literally frozen solid, the water is ice and well, ice cannot boil. So it can open a tiny pour for a second and grab that gas without turning into steam.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:55.060
Wow, okay. So this is not a plant that puts up with the cold. It is a plant that only works because it is cold?

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:02.900
That is the absolute heart of this whole design, yeah. But it definitely starts a huge debate about whether this is actually worth pursuing.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.460
Well, I really think it is worth another year of work. I mean, most things it needs already have a design ready to go, you know?

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.060
I hear you, but some of these needs are things nobody on Earth has even solved yet. I'm just not sure

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:16.860
it

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.700
makes sense. But the cheapest test is

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:25.220
literally just that child's drawing, right? Like a leaf in a jar with the air pumped out, which just takes one afternoon on a bench.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:37.160
Sure, but even if it survives, it would grow very little and definitely would not feed anyone. Plus, it dangerously stacks up changes the exact same way the first version did before a computer destroyed it.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:46.340
I guess we just have to let you decide if that risk is worth taking. But either way, every single version is looking for someone to read the design and a lab with a shelf to spare.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:55.400
Right, and the whole thing is written up at the MarsWorm website. That is a student project by Saanvi for the 3M Young Scientist Challenge 2027.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:07.800
Which really brings us right back to that picture of the leaf in the jar. One question asked five times, each answer changed by testing the one before it. None of them finished, and every one of them written up and waiting for someone to read it.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.760
It makes you wonder, though, if we can engineer a plant to survive by freezing on Mars, what

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.560
could that

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.000
do for failing crops right here on Earth?
